Negative control — `remove`'s addressability PRECONDITION (AC-11, fix 8a,
parity row L1, ruling R8a).

Reverts `remove`'s precondition to the pre-conversion INVERTED guard: the call
to the shared `isAddressable` predicate is replaced by the original
`isAuthenticated.value || !clientId.value`, which resolves TRUE for an
unauthenticated session with no client id and lets the DELETE go out at
`clients/undefined/addresses/{id}`. The mutation is the exact expression this
story removed, restored in place.

Declared AC — AC-11: "remove issues NO request for an unauthenticated session
with no client id". The read-back asserts the request-capture log is EMPTY;
asserting that a rejection surfaced does not discriminate, because the inverted
guard admits the call and it fails DOWNSTREAM.

No collateral measured. `setDefault` is deliberately left untouched, so AC-13's
read-backs stay GREEN under this mutant — `remove` and `setDefault` each carry
their own precondition and each earns its own control. AC-13's is the sibling
`client-address.default-guard.must-fail.patch`. The predicate ITSELF is covered
separately by `client-address.client-id-limb.must-fail.patch` (AC-3).

